= Chinese Licorice (甘草)

_The_ Chinese-species licorice — Glycyrrhiza uralensis _Fisch. — treated specifically as a Chinese pharmacological drug. The_ broader Glycyrrhiza genus _and the European-Indian_ G. glabra _are treated in_ licorice-root.typ; _the present entry focuses on what is distinctive about the Chinese species and the Chinese pharmacy tradition: the higher glycyrrhizin content, the_ raw vs. honey-fried _drug-form distinction (生甘草 vs. 炙甘草), the canonical_ 調和諸藥 (harmonizing-all-medicines) _role that makes 甘草 the most-used drug in classical Chinese formulas, the_ 十八反 (eighteen incompatibilities) _antagonist pairings, and the modern Chinese pharmacy applications including DGL development. Status: Chinese — fully canonical, among the most-used drugs of the entire Chinese pharmacopoeia. For Ayurvedic_ yaṣṭimadhu_, Greco-Roman_ glykyrrhiza / liquiritia_, and Tibb_ ʿirq al-sūs _treatments, see_ licorice-root.typ. _This entry treats them by reference and focuses on the Chinese register._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin:* _Glycyrrhiza uralensis_ Fisch. ex DC. — the Chinese / Mongolian / Siberian licorice species; the *Ural Mountains* of the species epithet refers to the broader cold-dry continental steppe range of its native distribution. Other Chinese-medicine-relevant species include *G. inflata* Batalin (also accepted in Chinese pharmacopoeia for 甘草) and *G. glabra* L. (the European-Indian species, accepted in some modern Chinese pharmacopoeia entries). The drug is the *dried root and rhizome*. For the broader genus and the etymology of _Glycyrrhiza_ from Greek _glykýrrhiza_ ("sweet root"), see _licorice-root.typ_.

*Chinese:* 甘草 (gāncǎo) — "sweet herb"; 甘 (gān, "sweet") + 草 (cǎo, "herb/grass"). The Chinese-character naming directly parallels the Greek-Latin-Sanskrit "sweet root / sweet stick" pattern — Greek _glykýrrhiza_, Sanskrit _yaṣṭimadhu_ ("sweet stick"), Chinese 甘草. Drug-form distinctions:
- *生甘草 (shēng gāncǎo, "raw licorice")* — the unprocessed dried root; cool / neutral; for heat-clearing and toxicity-resolution.
- *炙甘草 / 蜜炙甘草 (zhì gāncǎo, "honey-fried licorice")* — root sliced and fried with honey at moderate heat until coated and aromatic; warm; for qi-tonifying and middle-warming.
- *粉甘草 (fěn gāncǎo, "powdered licorice")* — finely powdered preparation.
- *甘草梢 (gāncǎo shāo, "licorice tail")* — the tail-end / smaller side-root portion, traditionally regarded as having specific lower-burner and urinary-track applications.

The 生 vs. 炙 drug-form distinction *parallels the ginger 生薑 vs. 乾薑 and the rehmannia 生地黃 vs. 熟地黃 distinctions* — the Chinese pharmacy paradigm of saṃskāra-creates-substance applied to licorice.#footnote[甘草 in _Shennong Bencao Jing_ (神農本草經), superior class (上品); full treatment in Li Shizhen, _Bencao Gangmu_ (本草綱目), 1578; systematized in Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Chinese Herbal Medicine: Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. (Eastland Press, 2004). The 生 / 炙 distinction is canonical from the _Shanghan Lun_ onward — Zhang Zhongjing's formulas systematically distinguish 甘草 vs. 炙甘草 by indication.]

For the Sanskrit, Greek-Latin, Persian-Arabic, and broader naming see _licorice-root.typ_.

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View — the substantive treatment

甘草 is in the _Shennong Bencao Jing_ superior class (上品) and appears in *one of the largest fractions of any single drug in the classical formulary* — by some counts in well over half of all Shanghan-Lun formulas. The drug has *three distinct roles* in Chinese pharmacy that justify its near-ubiquitous use:

1. *Principal drug for specific indications* (qi-vacuity, throat sores, drug toxicity, spasm).
2. *Adjuvant / moderating drug* — counterbalancing the harshness of stronger drugs.
3. *Harmonizing drug (調和諸藥)* — reconciling cold-hot, tonifying-purging, and other dialectical pairings within a single formula.

The third role — *調和諸藥* (tiáo hé zhū yào, "harmonizing all medicines") — is the most distinctive and is *unique to Chinese pharmacy among the three Eurasian classical traditions*. The reading: licorice as a "matchmaker" / "envoy" that reconciles the otherwise-incompatible actions of the other drugs in a multi-herb formula. This is what makes 甘草 the most-used drug in the entire pharmacopoeia.

*生甘草 (raw licorice):*

*Property (性 xìng / 氣 qì):* Cool (涼) to neutral (平). *Flavor (味 wèi):* Sweet (甘). *Channels entered (歸經 guījīng):* All twelve channels (classical), principally Heart (心), Lung (肺), Spleen (脾), Stomach (胃).

*Actions:*
- 清熱解毒 — *clears heat and resolves toxicity* — the cardinal action of raw licorice; for sore throat, carbuncles, drug poisoning, food poisoning, snake/insect bite (topical and internal).
- 潤肺止咳 — moistens the Lung and stops cough (for heat-pattern dry cough).
- 緩急止痛 — relaxes spasm and stops pain (with peony in 芍藥甘草湯).
- 調和諸藥 — harmonizes all medicines (the formula-construction role).

*炙甘草 (honey-fried licorice):*

*Property:* Warm (溫). *Flavor:* Sweet (甘). *Channels:* same with stronger Spleen-Stomach emphasis.

*Actions:*
- 補脾益氣 — *tonifies Spleen and augments qi* — the cardinal action; for qi-vacuity with shortness of breath, palpitations, fatigue.
- 復脈 — *restores the pulse* — the canonical use in 炙甘草湯 for Heart qi-and-blood-vacuity with arrhythmia.
- 緩急 — relaxes spasm.
- 調和諸藥 — harmonizing role (parallel to raw form).

*The 生 vs. 炙 distinction in clinical practice:* 生甘草 for heat-clearing / detoxification / acute throat sore; 炙甘草 for tonification / cardiac arrhythmia / qi-vacuity. The doctrine is strict in classical Chinese pharmacy; Western herbal practice has often blurred the distinction.

*Cautions:* see Modern Pharmacology for the *11β-HSD2 / mineralocorticoid effect* discussed in licorice-root.typ; classical Chinese: water-retention / 中滿 (zhōng mǎn, middle-fullness) caution for prolonged or high-dose use; the canonical caution applies precisely to the modern hypertension / hypokalemia syndrome — see licorice-root.typ for the full classical-modern convergence.

*The 十八反 (Eighteen Incompatibilities) — 甘草's antagonist pairings:* the famous classical-pharmacy mnemonic identifies 甘草 as antagonistic to four specific drugs:
- *甘遂 (gānsuì, _Euphorbia kansui_)* — euphorbia root, a strong purgative.
- *大戟 (dàjǐ, _Euphorbia pekinensis_ or _Knoxia valerianoides_)* — another strong purgative.
- *芫花 (yuánhuā, _Daphne genkwa_)* — daphne flower, a toxic purgative.
- *海藻 (hǎizǎo, _Sargassum_)* — seaweed.

These four drugs *should not be combined with 甘草 in the same formula* by classical doctrine. The modern mechanism is contested — some studies suggest the licorice + euphorbia combination enhances the toxicity of euphorbia compounds; the classical doctrine is universally observed in Chinese pharmacy practice regardless of mechanism.#footnote[十八反 (Eighteen Incompatibilities) classical mnemonic: enumerated in _Rúmén Shìqìn_ (儒門事親) and standard pharmacy references; Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed., appendix on incompatibilities. The 甘草 antagonist pairings are universal in Chinese pharmacy practice.]

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

See _licorice-root.typ_ for the substantive Ayurvedic treatment of _yaṣṭimadhu_ (= _Glycyrrhiza glabra_). The Chinese species _G. uralensis_ is *pharmacologically very similar but not identical* to _G. glabra_; the *glycyrrhizin content is somewhat higher* in _G. uralensis_, and the secondary flavonoid profile differs. Modern Ayurvedic / Unani Indian practice generally treats the two species interchangeably as _yaṣṭimadhu_; the formal Ayurvedic Pharmacopoeia of India recognizes _G. glabra_ as the canonical species. For practical purposes, the Ayurvedic placement of _G. uralensis_ follows _G. glabra_: madhura rasa, guru-snigdha guṇa, śīta vīrya, madhura vipāka, vāta-pitta-śāmaka, with the canonical _jīvanīya_ / _rasāyana_ / _sandhānīya_ / _vraṇa-ropaṇa_ / _cakṣuṣya_ / _kāsa-hara_ karma. See _licorice-root.typ_ for full treatment.

The *species-identification question* is real: when an Ayurvedic prescription specifies _yaṣṭimadhu_, the available commercial material in modern India is typically _G. glabra_ (the canonical Ayurvedic species); when a Chinese prescription specifies 甘草, the material is _G. uralensis_ (the canonical Chinese species). The cross-system substitutability is broadly accepted in modern pharmacy but is a real subject of variation in classical-text-faithful practice.

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

See _licorice-root.typ_ for the substantive Tibb treatment of _ʿirq al-sūs_. The Tibb tradition does not species-distinguish; the _G. uralensis_ Chinese species would be substitutable for _G. glabra_ in Tibb formulas by the modern Unani consensus. The classical Avicennan _Hot and Moist in the 1st degree_ classification, the *mufariḥ* / *muqawwī al-ṣadr* / *munaffith* actions, the *Sharbat-i Sūs* and *Maʿjūn-i Sūs* preparations all apply equally. The *Egyptian ʿirq sūs street beverage* (chilled licorice-root decoction sold in Cairo summers) is made from imported _G. glabra_ root in commerce; the same preparation made from _G. uralensis_ would be equivalent.

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫) — the substantive section

甘草 appears in *most foundational Chinese formulas*. Representative examples — see licorice-root.typ for some overlapping content:

*With 炙甘草 (honey-fried):*
- *炙甘草湯 / 復脈湯 (Zhì Gāncǎo Tāng / Fù Mài Tāng, "Honey-fried Licorice Decoction / Restore the Pulse Decoction")* — from the _Shanghan Lun_; 炙甘草 + 桂枝 + 生薑 + 大棗 + 麥門冬 + 麻仁 + 生地黃 + 阿膠 + 人參. *The foundational arrhythmia formula* of Chinese medicine; for Heart qi-and-blood-vacuity with palpitations and intermittent / knotted pulse.
- *四逆湯 (Sìnì Tāng, "Frigid Extremities Decoction")* — 炙甘草 + 附子 + 乾薑; *the canonical cold-collapse formula*; here 炙甘草 both tonifies the qi and *moderates the toxicity of aconite* (附子).
- *小建中湯 (Xiǎo Jiàn Zhōng Tāng, "Minor Construct the Middle Decoction")* — 炙甘草 + 桂枝 + 白芍 + 生薑 + 大棗 + 飴糖; for middle-burner qi-vacuity with abdominal cramping.
- *理中湯 (Lǐ Zhōng Tāng, "Regulate the Middle Decoction")* — 炙甘草 + 人參 + 白朮 + 乾薑; for Spleen-Stomach vacuity-cold.
- *桂枝湯 (Guìzhī Tāng, "Cinnamon Twig Decoction")* — the foundational exterior-harmonizing formula; 炙甘草 + 桂枝 + 白芍 + 生薑 + 大棗.

*With 生甘草 (raw):*
- *甘草湯 (Gāncǎo Tāng)* — single-herb decoction (生甘草 alone) for acute sore throat.
- *桔梗湯 (Jiégěng Tāng, "Platycodon Decoction")* — 生甘草 + 桔梗; foundational for throat infection.
- *白虎湯 (Bái Hǔ Tāng, "White Tiger Decoction")* — 生甘草 (with 石膏 + 知母 + 粳米); for Yangming-level interior heat.
- *銀翹散 (Yín Qiào Sǎn, "Lonicera and Forsythia Powder")* — 生甘草 in a Wen Bing exterior-warm-disease formula.

*With 甘草梢:* 導赤散 (Dǎo Chì Sǎn) and other lower-burner / urinary formulas use the tail-portion specifically.

*Standard doses:* 2–10 g in decoction; smaller (1–3 g) when acting as harmonizing adjuvant.#footnote[Formula sources: Zhang Zhongjing, _Shanghan Lun_ (傷寒論) and _Jīnguì Yàolüè_ (金匱要略) — the foundational 甘草 / 炙甘草 distinction; later formulas in _Tàipíng Huìmín Hé Jì Jú Fāng_ (太平惠民和劑局方); 銀翹散 and Wen Bing formulas from Wu Jutong, _Wēn Bìng Tiáo Biàn_ (溫病條辨, 1798). Compilation: Bensky & Barolet, _Chinese Herbal Medicine: Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed. (Eastland Press, 2009).]

