== Climate, Constitution & Regional Diet

_G. uralensis_ is principally a *cold-dry continental steppe* substance — its native distribution is Inner Mongolia (the canonical premium-production region), Xinjiang, Gansu, Heilongjiang, and the broader Mongolian-Siberian steppe / Eurasian steppe belt. The species is botanically distinct from _G. glabra_ (which is Mediterranean and Iranian) precisely because the two cover different climates within the genus. See _climates.typ_.

*Cold-dry continental steppe (Inner Mongolia, Xinjiang — the canonical _G. uralensis_ region).* The native climate carries cold harsh winters, hot dry summers, and an extreme seasonal range; the plant produces deep taproots that store glycyrrhizin and other actives as drought-and-cold tolerance compounds. In _ṛtucaryā_ terms the annual cycle is dominated by extreme winter Vāta load with summer Pitta peaks; in 中醫 terms the climate is 寒 (winter cold) on the Kidney with 燥 (summer dryness) on the Lung; in Tibb terms the constitution is *cold-dry* (sawdā' / melancholic-equivalent in winter, slight ṣafrā' in summer). *The traditional response*: 甘草's sweet-cooling-moistening profile (raw form) addresses summer dryness and Lung heat; the sweet-warming profile (honey-fried) addresses winter Vāta and Spleen vacuity-cold — the *raw / honey-fried distinction itself follows the annual climatic-constitutional cycle* in the production region.

*Application in subtropical-humid southern Chinese practice (where most Chinese pharmacy is now practiced):* 甘草 is imported as dried root into the southern climates and is used universally; the harmonizing role transcends climate. The honey-fried form's warming character is useful in the cool-damp winter; the raw form's heat-clearing character is useful in the damp-hot summer.

*Modern transposition (deśa-viruddha, Caraka Sūtra 26).* 甘草 exported globally as Chinese-medicine herb and as Western DGL / glycyrrhizin extract reaches every climate. The 調和諸藥 harmonizing role is climate-independent (a formula-construction principle, not a climatic correction). The therapeutic indications need climate consideration: high-dose long-course glycyrrhizin in hot-arid climates may exacerbate the 11β-HSD2 / mineralocorticoid effect because of baseline mineral-loss / dehydration concern in those climates; in cool-damp climates the same dose may be more tolerable. *DGL transcends the climate question* — the glycyrrhizin removal eliminates the mineralocorticoid concern and makes the substance climate-neutral.

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

See _licorice-root.typ_ for the substantive treatment of glycyrrhizin / glycyrrhetinic acid / 11β-HSD2 inhibition pharmacology; the flavonoid (liquiritin, isoliquiritin, glabridin) class; DGL; carbenoxolone; the safe daily dosage and risk groups.

*Specific to _G. uralensis_ versus _G. glabra_:*

- *Glycyrrhizin content*: _G. uralensis_ typically has *somewhat higher* glycyrrhizin content than _G. glabra_ (~2–10% by dry weight in _G. uralensis_; ~2–8% in _G. glabra_, with substantial regional variation). The *clinically relevant dose* in glycyrrhizin terms is the same; the species substitution at equal-weight is broadly equivalent.
- *Flavonoid profile*: _G. uralensis_ is somewhat richer in *liquiritin and isoliquiritin*; _G. glabra_ is richer in *glabridin* (which is essentially absent from _G. uralensis_). Glabridin has distinctive estrogenic and skin-depigmenting activity; the Chinese species lacks this particular property.
- *Honey-frying chemistry* (炙甘草): the honey-frying process modifies the flavonoid profile, introduces Maillard-reaction products, and concentrates the residual sugars; the 炙甘草 chemistry differs from the raw root, paralleling the dried-vs-fresh ginger and the prepared-vs-raw rehmannia distinctions.
- *Adjuvant role pharmacology*: the modern reading of 調和諸藥 — glycyrrhizin's role as a *bioavailability modifier* for co-administered compounds — is supported by mechanistic studies showing glycyrrhizin / glycyrrhetinic acid effects on intestinal transporters and CYP enzymes. The cross-system convergence with the classical "harmonizing" doctrine is partial-but-real.

*Cautions:* the *11β-HSD2 / mineralocorticoid effect* applies to both species equally; the dose-relevant figure is *glycyrrhizin mg/day*, not raw root mg/day. See _licorice-root.typ_ for the WHO/JECFA / EU SCF guidance and the risk-group details.

